const TPL={
 onboarding:f=>`Give me a repo-specific briefing on ${f}. Identify the exact files/functions involved, the mental model a new engineer needs, the surprising conventions, and the highest-risk assumption. End with the first three commands or files I should inspect next.`,
 planning:f=>`Plan ${f} before implementation. Research the current code path, propose two approaches with trade-offs, choose one, define rollback/verification, and split the work into reviewable steps. Do not write code until the plan is explicit.`,
 implementation:f=>`Implement ${f} with tight scope. First find the closest existing pattern in this repo and follow it. Add or update meaningful tests, handle error paths, avoid new dependencies unless justified, run the relevant checks, then summarize the diff and risks.`,
 debugging:f=>`Debug ${f} using evidence, not guesses. Reproduce the failure first, list ranked hypotheses, test the highest-likelihood one with the smallest probe, identify the root cause, apply the minimal fix, and leave a regression test that would fail on the old behavior.`,
 testing:f=>`Improve test coverage for ${f}. Start by naming the behavior in plain language, add tests for happy path, boundaries, failures, and one adversarial case. Prefer meaningful assertions over snapshots. Run the tests and explain what each test protects.`,
 refactor:f=>`Refactor ${f} without behavior changes. Establish the current safety net, add characterization tests if needed, make one structural improvement at a time, keep commits small, remove speculative abstractions, and prove behavior before/after with tests or targeted checks.`,
 git:f=>`Handle the git workflow for ${f}. Inspect the diff/history, keep each change logically separated, write conventional commits with the why in the body, run the pre-push checklist, and prepare a PR description that explains risk, testing, and reviewer focus areas.`,
 review:f=>`Review ${f} like a staff engineer. Prioritize correctness, security, data privacy, reliability, and maintainability. Severity-tag findings with file:line, explain impact, suggest concrete fixes, and if it is clean, state the specific risks you checked.`,
 docs:f=>`Document ${f} from reality, not memory. Verify commands or code paths where possible, write for a new engineer, include examples, note gotchas, and flag any area where documentation cannot be verified from the current repo.`,
 automation:f=>`Automate ${f} safely. Define inputs, allowed tools, deny rules, timeout, logs, and a self-checking definition of done. Prefer read-only or draft output first. If the automation can affect users, require explicit confirmation before irreversible actions.`,
 config:f=>`Improve Claude Code configuration for ${f}. Keep tools least-privilege, make instructions specific to this repo, add deterministic hooks only where they reduce risk, test the configuration, and document how the team can verify it on a fresh clone.`,
 workflow:f=>`Run a disciplined session for ${f}. Restate the goal, constraints, and definition of done; identify ambiguity; work in checkpoints; externalize decisions; stop when scope drifts; and leave a handoff that a fresh session can continue.`
};

